# Caldera Repeater — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Repeater, the cheap workhorse tower of the
Holdfast — a slim brass post rising to a rotating drum of many short iron barrels, like
a rotary gun — as a 3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with
`voxel-anim` (painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads
unmistakably as this fast, light emplacement, ticks its drum over on its own, and cycles
its barrels in a burst on demand. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the Repeater is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list,
joint placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a tower whose barrel drum spins
needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the tower, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the barrels point toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front) at rest. Up is +y.
- The Repeater is a **static emplacement founded on the ground**: its base sits flat on
  the ground at `y = 0` and does not move. Build it symmetric about the lengthwise vertical
  centerplane.
- The Repeater is deliberately **slim and medium-height** — a slender post, not a bulky
  one, carrying the drum up where it can traverse. It is the cheap, fast, light workhorse;
  nothing about it should read as heavy, squat, or massive.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Repeater (a leg of the post
  already under the drum, the barrels already clustered around their axis).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, barrels, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto the accent collar at run time to show the tower's
upgrade level (see *The accent region*); baking any of them in makes a base-level tower
look permanently upgraded.

## What the Repeater is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Repeater must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **slim brass post** founded flat on the ground, rising to carry the drum aloft. It
  is slender, not bulky — the light, cheap workhorse. Its shadowed seams and undersides
  are brass dark.
- A **rotating drum of many short barrels** at the top — a cluster of short iron muzzles
  arranged around a single spinning axis, like a rotary gun. The **many short barrels** are
  the Repeater's whole tell: a reviewer must be able to name it from that cluster of
  muzzles alone.
- A **collar ring** where the drum meets the post — the accent region below.

The Repeater is a **weapon of the Holdfast — brass-and-iron machinery, not a creature**. The
tell is the **drum of many short barrels** crowning a slim post: nothing about this tower is
squat, needle-thin, or barrel-less.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the post and
drum, how many barrels ring the axis and how they are shaped, how the collar is formed, and
how you break the Repeater into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape; the test rewards
a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the Repeater and animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the tower's **upgrade level**, by finding
every voxel of one reserved color and repainting it (brass dark at level 0, steel at level
1, gold at level 2), so one model serves all three levels. That reserved color is the
**verdigris accent** `#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **collar ring** where the barrel drum meets the top of the post — and only
  that — in `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a
  seam. Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- The collar must be **one contiguous ring** and clearly **visible from more than one
  angle** — that is how a player reads a tower's upgrade level at a glance across the
  battlefield.

Everything else — the post, the drum, the barrels, the recesses — keeps its own color and is
never repainted. Get this wrong and every Repeater in the game looks the same level.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the
period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion reads right — a slow, steady tick-over at idle and a fast, snappy cycle when firing.

- **`idle`** — the tick-over (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`, `auto_play = true`). The
  barrel drum turns slowly and continuously on its own, with no caller, so the tower reads
  as live and powered even when it is not firing. Only the drum turns; the post and the
  ground base stay fixed. It loops seamlessly.
- **`fire`** — the burst (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The drum spins up fast
  and the barrels cycle, one muzzle coming round after another in a rapid burst, reading as
  a light, fast-firing rotary gun rather than a single heavy shot. The post and base stay
  fixed; only the drum spins.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a faint
sway of a heat wisp), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle` and `fire`
animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt
each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation; run
`voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png` previews)
between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the post stands under the drum,
the barrels cluster evenly around their axis, the collar rings the join unbroken, and the
animations read with the right snap. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits
the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty
(`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms>
--animation <name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion).
